  prose(<<~'CANONICAL_PROSE', origin: :published)
    ## Physical Characteristics
    
    A submarine convergence zone where three pressure-depths collide in a vertical spiral beneath the frozen sea. Water temperature holds near sixty degrees — warm enough that ice forms only in thin crusts that dissolve within hours, leaving the surface perpetually slick. The convergence generates anomalous current patterns: fish caught in the spiral swim tightening circles until exhaustion, their bodies surfacing weeks later coated in frost that resists the ambient heat, as though the cold operates on a different principle than the warmth.
    
    Debris rises continuously through the convergence point — bone fragments, corroded metal, material that predates the Berg — suspended in mineral-rich water that runs rust-red and iron-bitter. Orca shamans call this taste the blood of the dead god whose corpse drifts on deep currents beneath the site. Penguin witnesses who have approached close enough to perceive the surface — none invited, all trespassers — report not whale-song but a lower frequency, one that produces pain in the beak and a sensation of cranial pressure.
    
    ## Theological Significance
    
    The Current is the holiest site in orca religious practice. The Wake-Singers, the shaman cult dedicated to waking the dead god through accumulated blood sacrifice, conduct their most consequential rituals here and guard the location absolutely. The orcas have killed for lesser intrusions than approaching the site uninvited. Some Wake-Singer shamans have not returned from their own pilgrimages — a detail the oral traditions preserve with the specificity I have learned to distrust.
    
    Orca accounts describe the dead god's submerged flesh twitching in rhythmic patterns, a pulse that some shamans identify as the source of the Current's anomalous warmth. The Dreaming Tooth, a massive artifact carved from the dead god's jaw by the first orca shaman, is kept at the convergence point to focus and amplify ritual song. Grandmother Deep, the eldest living orca and theological authority of the Wake-Singers, visits when the Current's reach extends toward the Berg's foundations — a phenomenon she reportedly describes as growing stronger.
    
    ## Historical Role
    
    During the Clever Ice Age, the Current served as the sole channel of liquid water in the frozen sea, maintained by Grandmother Deep's nine-day circuits beneath the Berg. When she surfaced at its heart, thermal vents surged and krill bloomed in the narrow band of open water her passage held. The ice-memory in the Foundation Depths records thermal signatures consistent with sustained geothermal activity at a single point during the deep-freeze strata, though I cannot confirm the nine-day interval from ice alone.
    
    Berg-side settlements near the Current's influence do not speak its name. The common euphemisms — "the warm place," "where the singers go to drown" — carry the residue of long familiarity with something residents prefer not to examine directly.
    
    ## Custodianship
    
    The ☽'ofund' Guild, an orca faction, seized control of the site from Wake-Singer remnants during the Orca Incursion without presenting sealed authority — a jurisdictional irregularity that no surviving record addresses. The Guild maintains only a skeleton watch: sufficient to deter outsiders, insufficient to claim genuine dominion over what occupies the convergence.
    
    ## The Abyss Collapse Tide and After
    
    A thermal seam fractured beneath the Current during the catastrophic Abyss Collapse Tide, flooding the abyss with superheated water that killed entire orca pods and disrupted trade routes both penguin civilizations depended upon. Superheated steam forced through channels that had carried only cold draft for generations. The warm place grew warmer.
    
    The Nightshelf Remembrancers, the deep-ice chroniclers of Nightfall Shelf, describe the Current as the point where the Berg's memory leaks into the present — artifacts and remains surfacing through the convergence as though the deep were expelling what it could no longer contain.
    
    ## Extended Manifestations
    
    The Current's signature phenomena — iron taste, spiral patterning, warmth beyond thermal explanation — have appeared in deep-ice convergence points far from the seafloor, notably in tunnels beneath the Nightshelf settlement of Sleek☽ Caemomot where the Ofundis∴flux tore open the eastern vein. The Nightshelf has no designation for what inhabits those deep convergences. The mirrored properties suggest the Current's influence propagates through the ice itself, reaching places no orca has ever swum.
  CANONICAL_PROSE
